"name gets a certificate of 12 hours, renewed every 8 hours by the same "
"thread, with no restart. Several nodes that share the storage use one "
"root. One frontend may mix public and internal names. ``tls: { auto: "
"[\"www.example.org\"], issuer: \"internal\" }`` sends a public name to "
"the local authority, for a staging host or a private network. ``issuer: "
"\"acme\"`` with an internal name is refused when the file is checked, "
"with a hint. With a ``directory`` of our own, internal names go to that "
"authority, and addresses still to the local one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:682
msgid "We may limit what the root can sign for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:684
msgid ""
"global: { acme: { internal: { constraints: [\"corp.internal\", "
"\"localhost\", \"10.0.0.0/8\"] } } },"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:688
msgid ""
"``constraints`` lists DNS suffixes and address ranges, at most 32. A "
"suffix permits its own name and every name below it. Sibuna writes the "
"list into the root as a critical name constraint when it creates the "
"root. A client that trusts the root then refuses any certificate under it "
"for another name, whoever signed it. So a stolen root key cannot be used "
"against ``bank.example``. A list with suffixes only permits no address, "
"and a list with ranges only permits no host name. Without the key the "
"root may sign for every name, which ``issuer: \"internal\"`` on a public "
"name needs. ``sibuna check`` refuses a local name outside the list with "
"the diagnostic ``NAME OUTSIDE CONSTRAINTS``. The list belongs to the "
"root: Sibuna never rewrites a root. When the file lists other constraints "
"than the root has, the local authority fails with ``ACME011`` and a hint. "
"A longer list needs a new root. We remove ``root.pem``, ``root.crt`` and "
"``intermediate.pem`` in the directory ``local``, and we install the new "
"root in the clients."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:690
msgid ""
"A certificate of the local authority lives 12 hours, so a stolen key is "
"useful until that certificate expires, and ``lifetime`` shortens this. To "
"stop serving a name we remove it from the file. When a key is lost before "
"its certificate expires, we revoke the certificate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:692
msgid ""
"$ sibuna ctl /run/sibuna/edge.sock revoke cert db.internal\n"
"revoked serial 5f0c1a7be2d94410 name db.internal\n"
"# The revocation lists are written. A served certificate with this serial "
"number is replaced now."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:698
msgid ""
"``revoke cert`` takes a name that the local authority issues for on this "
"node, or a serial number in hexadecimal digits, as ``openssl x509 -noout "
"-serial`` prints it. The name gets a new certificate with a new key at "
"once, with no restart. The serial form also revokes a certificate that "
"this node no longer holds. A word that names nothing fails with "
"``ADM013``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:700
msgid ""
"The local authority keeps a revocation list in the directory ``local`` of "
"the storage: ``crl.pem``, and ``crl.der`` for a tool that reads DER. "
"Sibuna writes the list at the first internal name, also when nothing is "
"revoked, and writes it again after every revocation and at least every 12 "
"hours. A list is valid for 24 hours. A client does not find the list by "
"itself. We give the file to each verifier that shall honour a revocation, "
"and we give it again within a day, for example with a timer that copies "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:702
msgid ""
"openssl verify -crl_check -CAfile root.crt -untrusted intermediate.pem "
"-CRLfile crl.pem leaf.pem\n"
"ssl_crl /etc/nginx/sibuna-crl.pem;              # nginx, for client "
"certificates of this authority\n"
"SSLCARevocationFile /etc/apache2/sibuna-crl.pem"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:708
msgid ""
"A verifier that is not given the list accepts a revoked certificate until "
"it expires. ``crl.pem`` may hold two lists: the intermediate is replaced "
"every few days, and the one before it signs a list of its own while its "
"certificates live. When the list cannot be written, the log has "
"``ACME016`` with the path. When the storage loses its root, Sibuna makes "
"a new root at the next start, does not serve the stored certificates of "
"the old root, and issues new ones at once. The local authority issues for "
"this daemon and for the nodes that share its storage. For other machines "
"it can be an ACME server, which is off unless we turn it on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:710
msgid ""
"**An ACME server for the internal network.** With ``internal.server`` "
"another machine obtains a certificate of the local authority with an ACME "
"client: certbot, lego, acme.sh, or a second Sibuna whose ``directory`` is "
"our URL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:712
msgid ""
"\"global\": { \"acme\": { \"internal\": {\n"
"    \"constraints\": [\"corp.internal\", \"10.0.0.0/8\"],\n"
"    \"server\": { \"url\": \"https://ca.corp.internal/acme\",\n"
"                \"allow\": [\"corp.internal\", \"10.0.0.0/8\"] } } } },\n"
"\"frontends\": { \"ca\": { \"proto\": \"http\", \"binds\": "
"[\"10.0.0.5:443\"],\n"
"    \"tls\": { \"auto\": [\"ca.corp.internal\"] }, \"default_backend\": "
"\"app\" } }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:721
msgid ""
"``url`` is what we give to the clients, and its directory is "
"``URL/directory``. The server has no port of its own. It answers on the "
"TLS frontends, for requests whose host and path are those of ``url``, so "
"one frontend must serve that host. In the example the local authority "
"issues the certificate of ``ca.corp.internal`` itself. An ``http`` URL is "
"accepted for ``localhost`` and loopback addresses only. ``allow`` lists "
"the DNS suffixes that a client may order under and the address ranges "
"that a validation may connect into. It needs one of each. ``http_port`` "
"is the port of the validation, 80 unless we state it. A change of "
"``server`` needs a restart."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:723
msgid ""
"A client must trust the root before it can speak to the server, because "
"the server is behind a certificate of the same authority. We install the "
"root on the client as shown below, or we name it to the client."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:725
msgid ""
"sibuna ctl /run/sibuna/edge.sock show ca > sibuna-root.crt\n"
"certbot certonly --standalone -d db.corp.internal \\\n"
"    --server https://ca.corp.internal/acme/directory\n"
"REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=sibuna-root.crt certbot ...      # when the root is "
"not installed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:732
msgid ""
"The server proves a name with HTTP-01 and nothing else. It resolves the "
"name, connects to the first address inside a range of ``allow`` on "
"``http_port``, and asks for ``/.well-known/acme-challenge/TOKEN``. It "
"follows no redirect and contacts no address outside ``allow``. So it "
"issues host names only: no wildcard and no IP address. A certificate "
"lives for ``internal.lifetime``, 12 hours unless we change it and two "
"days at most. A client that renews a month before the end, as certbot "
"does, must be told to renew every few hours, for example with a timer "
"that runs ``certbot renew --force-renewal``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:734
msgid "What the server does not have"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:738
msgid "Missing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:739
msgid "What we do instead"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:740
msgid "A list of clients, external account binding."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:741
msgid ""
"Everybody who reaches the URL and can answer on port 80 of an address in "
"``allow`` for a name under ``allow`` obtains a certificate. We keep the "
"URL inside the network, we keep ``allow`` narrow, and we set "
"``internal.constraints``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:742
msgid "A limit per client address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:743
msgid ""
"The server holds 1,024 orders, 16 for one account key, and 4,096 nonces. "
"A client that fills them makes others wait up to an hour. The answer is "
"written before the firewall, so its limits do not apply."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:744
msgid "Revocation by the client."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:745
msgid ""
"``revoke cert SERIAL`` on the admin socket. The serial is on the "
"certificate of the client: the daemon logs the names of what it issues "
"and keeps no list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:746
msgid "Orders that survive a restart, or that several nodes share."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:747
msgid ""
"Orders live in memory for at most one hour. After a restart a client "
"orders again. Behind several nodes, a client must reach one node for one "
"order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:748
msgid ""
"Wildcards, IP addresses, DNS-01, TLS-ALPN-01, key rollover, renewal "
"information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:749
msgid ""
"Nothing. A wildcard for an internal name comes from ``tls.auto`` on this "
"daemon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:751
msgid ""
"``URL/crl`` serves the revocation list of the present intermediate in "
"DER, for a verifier that fetches lists over HTTPS. The log has one line "
"for each validation and each certificate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:753
msgid ""
"ACME000: ACME server: db.corp.internal validated for a client\n"
"ACME000: ACME server: certificate issued to a client for "
"db.corp.internal\n"
"ACME000: ACME server: db.corp.internal not validated for a client: The "
"answer has the status 404, not 200."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:759
msgid ""
"This server was tested with the ACME client of Sibuna. Other clients were "
"not run against it yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:761
msgid ""
"A client that connects to an IP address sends no server name. Such a "
"handshake receives the certificate of the first address under ``auto``, "
"and that certificate lists every address under ``auto``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:763
msgid ""
"A client trusts these certificates once it trusts the root. Sibuna never "
"changes a trust store. It shows us the root, and we install it where we "
"want it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:765
msgid ""
"sibuna ctl /run/sibuna/edge.sock show ca > sibuna-root.crt\n"
"\n"
"curl --cacert sibuna-root.crt https://localhost/                      # "
"one command, no installation\n"
"\n"
"# Debian, Ubuntu\n"
"sudo cp sibuna-root.crt /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/sibuna-root.crt "
"&& sudo update-ca-certificates\n"
"# Fedora, RHEL\n"
"sudo cp sibuna-root.crt /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/ && sudo "
"update-ca-trust\n"
"# macOS\n"
"sudo security add-trusted-cert -d -r trustRoot -k "
"/Library/Keychains/System.keychain sibuna-root.crt\n"
"# Windows, in an administrator shell\n"
"certutil -addstore -f Root sibuna-root.crt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:780
msgid ""
"The lines of ``show ca`` that start with ``#`` name the file and its "
"expiry. The rest is the PEM text, which every tool above reads. Firefox "
"and Java keep trust stores of their own. The file ``local/root.crt`` in "
"the storage holds the same certificate. It has mode 0644 and its "
"directory mode 0755, so another user can read it when the storage "
"directory lets them pass. Sibuna creates the storage directory with mode "
"0700 and never changes it afterwards, so by default only ``show ca`` "
"reaches the root. Whoever can read ``local/root.pem`` in the storage can "
"sign towards the clients that trust this root, for any name that "
"``constraints`` permits, so we install the root only on machines we "
"control, and we protect the storage like a private key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:782
msgid ""
"**On demand.** Sometimes we cannot list the names: customers point their "
"own domains at us, or each tenant gets a subdomain. A frontend with "
"``on_demand: true`` obtains the certificate at the first handshake that "
"names a host. We must say which hosts may get one, and the line "
"``global`` does that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:784
msgid ""
"fn edge() -> Map<Value> {\n"
"    return {\n"
"        global: { acme: { on_demand: { allow: [\"apps.example.org\"] } } "
"},\n"
"        frontends: {\n"
"            web: { binds: [\"0.0.0.0:443\"], tls: { on_demand: true } },\n"
"            plain: { binds: [\"0.0.0.0:80\"] },\n"
"        },\n"
"        backends: { app: { servers: [\"10.0.0.1:80\"] } },\n"
"    }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:797
msgid ""
"The first client that connects to ``shop.apps.example.org`` waits a few "
"seconds while Sibuna orders the certificate, and is then served with it. "
"Every later client finds it. ``auto`` may list names on the same "
"frontend, and it may be absent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:799
msgid ""
"client                         Sibuna                               "
"certificate authority\n"
"  | -- ClientHello: shop.apps.example.org -->  no certificate yet\n"
"  |                              guards pass: order ------------------>  "
"|\n"
"  |          (the handshake waits, at most `wait` seconds)  <-- validate  "
"|\n"
"  |                              certificate  <------------------------  "
"|\n"
"  | <----- handshake goes on with the new certificate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:808
msgid ""
"A guard is required. Without one, anybody who can reach the listener "
"could make us order certificates for any name that points at us, and "
"spend the limits of the authority. ``sibuna check`` and the start refuse "
"such a file with the title ``ON DEMAND NEEDS A GUARD``. There are two "
"guards, and we may use both."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:810
msgid ""
"``allow`` lists suffixes. ``apps.example.org`` allows that name and every "
"name below it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:811
msgid ""
"``ask`` names a URL of ours. Sibuna sends ``GET URL?domain=NAME`` and "
"allows the name on a status from 200 to 299. Any other status refuses it, "
"and so does no answer within 5 seconds. We remember a refusal for 60 "
"seconds, and we ask again at each renewal. The endpoint should answer "
"from a table of known customers, on the loopback interface or a private "
"network."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:813
msgid ""
"global: { acme: { on_demand: { ask: "
"\"http://127.0.0.1:9000/tls-allowed\", rate: 30 } } },"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:817
msgid "Members of ``global.acme.on_demand``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:824
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "``mac``"
msgid "``ask``"
msgstr "``mac``"

#: ../../operations.rst:826
msgid "An ``http`` or ``https`` URL without a query. It decides each name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:827
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "``posw``"
msgid "``allow``"
msgstr "``posw``"

#: ../../operations.rst:829
msgid ""
"One to 64 suffixes. A name outside them is never asked about and never "
"ordered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:830
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "``mac``"
msgid "``rate``"
msgstr "``mac``"

#: ../../operations.rst:831
msgid "10"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:832
msgid ""
"New certificates per hour, from 1 to 1000. The first ``rate`` names are "
"served at once, and then one more every :math:`3600 / rate` seconds. A "
"certificate that the storage already has costs nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:833
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "``mac``"
msgid "``names``"
msgstr "``mac``"

#: ../../operations.rst:834
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "``100``"
msgid "100"
msgstr "``100``"

#: ../../operations.rst:835
msgid "The most names held on demand at once, from 1 to 10000."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:837
msgid "20"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:838
msgid ""
"The longest time a handshake waits for its certificate, in seconds, from "
"1 to 120."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:840
msgid ""
"A name on demand is a host name. A wildcard and an IP address are never "
"obtained on demand. The issuer follows the name as for a listed name: a "
"public name goes to the ACME authority, and an internal name such as "
"``app.localhost`` goes to the local authority, which answers at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:842
msgid ""
"**What a client sees.** A handshake for a name that a guard refuses does "
"not wait. It gets what any unknown name gets: the first certificate of "
"``crt`` when the frontend has one, and a TLS alert otherwise. A handshake "
"whose order takes longer than ``wait`` gets the same, and the order goes "
"on, so a later handshake finds the certificate. One order runs at a time, "
"so many new names at once are served one after the other. HTTP/3 never "
"waits: a QUIC handshake uses the certificate once a TCP handshake has "
"obtained it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:844
msgid ""
"**Keeping and releasing.** A name on demand has a line in ``show certs`` "
"with ``on-demand`` in the column ``origin``, and it is renewed in the "
"background like a listed name. At its renewal Sibuna asks two questions. "
"Did a handshake name it since the certificate was obtained? Does ``ask`` "
"still allow it? If either answer is no, the name is released: it is no "
"longer served or renewed, and its line leaves ``show certs``. The log "
"says which. A name whose first order fails is released at once and "
"refused until its retry delay has passed. The next handshake after that "
"asks again. A restart forgets the names and keeps the certificates: the "
"first handshake for each loads its certificate from the storage, with no "
"order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:846
msgid ""
"**More than one authority.** An authority can be down, or can refuse us "
"for a while. We name a second one, and Sibuna asks it in the same attempt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:848
msgid ""
"fn edge() -> Map<Value> {\n"
"    return {\n"
"        global: { acme: {\n"
"            email: \"ops@example.org\",\n"
"            fallback: [\n"
"                {\n"
"                    directory: \"https://acme.zerossl.com/v2/DV90\",\n"
"                    email: \"ops@example.org\",\n"
"                    eab: { kid: \"kid-from-the-dashboard\", key_file: "
"\"/etc/sibuna/zerossl.key\" },\n"
"                },\n"
"            ],\n"
"        } },\n"
"        frontends: {\n"
"            web: { binds: [\"0.0.0.0:443\"], tls: { auto: "
"[\"example.org\"] } },\n"
"            plain: { binds: [\"0.0.0.0:80\"] },\n"
"        },\n"
"        backends: { app: { servers: [\"10.0.0.1:80\"] } },\n"
"    }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:870
msgid ""
"The keys ``directory``, ``email``, ``ca_file`` and ``eab`` of "
"``global.acme`` describe the first authority, here Let's Encrypt by "
"default. Each member of ``fallback`` has the same four keys, and "
"``directory`` is required there. One attempt for a name asks the "
"authorities in order and stops at the first certificate. The log names "
"each authority that failed and the one that is asked next. When all fail, "
"the attempt fails once and is tried again after the usual delay, with the "
"first authority first. A certificate is stored under the authority that "
"issued it, and at a start we serve the stored certificate that expires "
"last. ``show certs`` names the issuer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:872
msgid ""
"Some authorities issue only to an account that they know already. They "
"hand out a key identifier and a MAC key, the *external account binding*. "
"We write the identifier into ``kid`` and the key, in base64url as the "
"authority shows it, into a file that only the daemon's user can read. "
"Sibuna signs the registration with it once. The key is never logged. An "
"authority that wants a binding and gets none fails with ``ACME012`` "
"before we send it anything signed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:874
msgid ""
"All authorities share one account key, the one in the directory of the "
"first authority. So every node that shares the storage answers an HTTP-01 "
"challenge of any of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:876
msgid ""
"**When a certificate is renewed.** Without other knowledge we renew at "
"two thirds of the lifetime. An authority that supports renewal "
"information (RFC 9773) may know better: it spreads the load of its "
"clients, and it asks for an early renewal before it revokes a "
"certificate. Sibuna asks the authority for each certificate, every six "
"hours or as often as the authority says, between one hour and one day. "
"The answer is a window. We take one time in it, the same on every node "
"and after every restart, and ``show certs`` shows it in the column "
"``renews``. Two bounds are ours. We never renew later than four fifths of "
"the lifetime, whatever the authority says. And a certificate that we just "
"obtained is not renewed in its first two minutes. The new order tells the "
"authority which certificate it replaces. ``ari: false`` turns the "
"questions off. An authority without renewal information is not asked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:878
msgid ""
"**OCSP stapling.** Some authorities put the URL of an OCSP responder into "
"their certificates. For such a certificate Sibuna asks the responder "
"whether the certificate is still good, checks the signature of the answer "
"against the issuer, and staples the answer to every handshake. A client "
"then needs no connection to the responder. Sibuna asks again at the "
"middle of the validity of each answer, keeps the answer beside the "
"certificate, and staples it again after a restart. An answer whose "
"validity ends is no longer stapled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:880
msgid ""
"certificate installed --> ask the responder --> good     --> staple, ask "
"again at half of the validity\n"
"                                             --> revoked  --> staple "
"nothing, renew the certificate\n"
"                                             --> failure  --> serve "
"without a staple, ask again later"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:886
msgid ""
"When the responder says ``revoked``, Sibuna logs it and renews the "
"certificate at once. A certificate that it obtained a moment ago waits a "
"short time first, so a responder cannot cause a loop of orders. A "
"responder that fails changes nothing for the clients: the certificate is "
"served without a staple, the failure is logged with ``ACME013``, and the "
"question is repeated with growing delays. A certificate that names no "
"responder gets no staple, and nobody is asked. Let's Encrypt names none "
"since 2025. ``ocsp: false`` turns stapling off. Only ``http`` responders "
"are asked. A certificate from ``crt`` is stapled from a file, as the "
"section on certificates says."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:888
msgid "**Looking at the state.** The admin socket lists the certificates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:890
msgid "sibuna ctl /run/sibuna/edge.sock show certs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:894
msgid ""
"name                  via     issuer                                "
"expires              renews               state origin    ocsp error\n"
"*.example.org         dns-01  R11                                   "
"2027-02-22T10:00:00Z 2027-01-23T10:00:00Z ready listed    good -\n"
"example.org           http-01 R11                                   "
"2027-02-22T10:00:00Z 2027-01-23T10:00:00Z ready listed    good -\n"
"localhost             local   Sibuna_Local_Authority_-_Intermediate "
"2026-11-24T22:00:00Z 2026-11-24T18:00:00Z ready listed    -    -\n"
"shop.apps.example.org http-01 R11                                   "
"2027-02-22T10:00:00Z 2027-01-23T10:00:00Z ready on-demand good -\n"
"www.example.org       http-01 -                                     -     "
"               2026-11-24T10:04:00Z retry listed    -    VALIDATION "
"FAILED (ACME005)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:903
msgid ""
"``via`` says how the certificate is obtained: the challenge, or ``local`` "
"for the local authority. ``origin`` is ``listed`` for a name under "
"``auto`` and ``on-demand`` for a name that a handshake asked for. "
"``ocsp`` is ``good`` while a response is stapled, ``revoked`` when the "
"responder said so, and ``-`` otherwise. ``state`` is ``pending`` before "
"the first attempt, ``ordering`` during an order, ``waiting`` while "
"another node holds the lock, ``retry`` after a failure, and ``ready`` "
"otherwise. ``renews`` is the time of the next order. ``show stats`` and "
"the metrics endpoint add four series: "
"``sibuna_edge_acme_certificates_managed``, "
"``sibuna_edge_acme_certificates_served``, "
"``sibuna_edge_acme_nearest_expiry_seconds`` and "
"``sibuna_edge_acme_failures_total``. An alert on the third one tells us "
"when renewal has been failing for too long."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:905
msgid ""
"**Troubleshooting.** Each failure is written to standard error with a "
"code, the reason of the authority and a hint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:907
msgid ""
"-- VALIDATION FAILED (ACME005) "
"----------------------------------------------\n"
"\n"
"No new certificate for `www.example.org`. The authority could not "
"validate the name with http-01: 203.0.113.9: Connection refused. The "
"listeners keep serving. Next attempt at 2026-11-24T10:04:00Z.\n"
"\n"
"Hint: Point the DNS record of the name at this node, and let the "
"authority reach port 80 (HTTP-01) or port 443 (TLS-ALPN-01) from the "
"Internet."
msgstr ""

